          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does osteoarthritis guidance describe as core treatment before anything else?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis guidance places **exercise and other non-drug measures first**, before any medicine. The rheumatology and orthopedic society guidance (titled \"Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management and the sequence of treatment options\") calls this the core recommendation. It says every major body places non-drug management first. The program it describes combines strengthening exercises around the affected joint, range-of-movement work, and aerobic activity, ideally with supervision at least at first. The same document says exercise helps regardless of how severe the X-ray changes are, how old the person is, or how much pain they have at the start.\n\nThe guidance also describes the evidence strength. It says structured exercise has the strongest and most consistent evidence of any intervention for knee and hip osteoarthritis, with effects comparable to drug options and a better safety profile.\n\nMedicines come after exercise, as add-ons rather than replacements. The same document describes topical anti-inflammatory creams or gels as the preferred first drug for knee and hand osteoarthritis. It also covers oral anti-inflammatory drugs, steroid injections for short-term flares, and duloxetine for widespread pain. It says opioids are not recommended, and that glucosamine and chondroitin are not recommended by most bodies.\n\n**On disagreement:** the osteoarthritis document is flagged as having a section on where guidance differs, but the text of that section was not returned to me. I can't tell you what the authorities disagree about, so I won't guess. A clinician or the full guideline document would be the place to check.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
